WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.672
You have seen this. A closed laundromat at night, magenta and cyan neon pooling on the linoleum.

00:00:07.022 --> 00:00:13.571
A washer drum stands in that light. At the enamel the shadow is sharp. Farther along the aisle it goes soft.

00:00:13.921 --> 00:00:18.657
That soft edge is a penumbra. It is the same shape you see next to a streetlamp.

00:00:19.857 --> 00:00:25.212
So here is the puzzle. Why is the shadow sharp next to the drum and soft farther away?

00:00:25.562 --> 00:00:28.485
And how can a cheap method get both, in one pass?

00:00:28.835 --> 00:00:37.411
The usual answer is to march hundreds of rays from every empty pixel across the whole room. That works. It is also very expensive.

00:00:38.611 --> 00:00:45.800
We stand at five spots east of the drum, from 8 to 80 pixels away, and look across the shadow edge at each one.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:50.801
The spacing between probes should stay on the order of the distance to the occluder.

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:55.524
The angle between rays should stay on the order of one over that distance.

00:00:55.874 --> 00:01:16.759
The spacing we can get away with grows, from 28 pixels near the drum to 57 at 80 pixels, about twice as wide. But the angle test does not move. Every spot needs 128 rays, because the whole room's neon, not this one drum, sets the angular detail. So gate 4 fails, honestly.

00:01:17.959 --> 00:01:21.159
Radiance cascades store that tradeoff as stacked shells.

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:27.589
Each cascade doubles the gap between probes, which in two dimensions means a quarter as many probes.

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:38.201
It also uses four times as many rays, and four times as long an interval. The product of probes and rays stays 204800.

00:01:38.551 --> 00:01:44.033
Ray number k of a probe points at two pi times k plus a half, over the ray count.

00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:51.953
A radiance interval is the light you see looking along a ray, counting only what sits between two distances.

00:01:52.303 --> 00:02:03.909
If something opaque sits in that segment, transmittance is zero and you take that surface light. If the segment is empty, transmittance is one and the interval carries no light of its own.

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:13.347
Radiance of the combined interval equals radiance of the near interval, plus the transmittance of the near interval times radiance of the far interval.

00:02:13.697 --> 00:02:18.646
Transmittance of the combined interval equals the product of the two transmittances.

00:02:19.846 --> 00:02:23.323
And that is the aha. Merging is looking through a window.

00:02:23.673 --> 00:02:27.129
If the near interval hits a wall, the far light is blocked.

00:02:27.479 --> 00:02:34.455
If the near interval is empty, you see whatever the next cascade stored, as if you had cast a much longer ray.

00:02:34.805 --> 00:02:39.243
The sharp contact and the soft aisle are the same rule, at two distances.

00:02:40.443 --> 00:02:42.981
Here is the cost, measured on this laundry.

00:02:43.331 --> 00:02:59.779
The reference casts 256 full length rays from every empty pixel. That is 180313088 rays and 1614796359 steps.

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:11.415
Cascades instead march 1228800 short intervals on six shells, in 3631190 steps.

00:03:11.765 --> 00:03:25.439
The ratio of intervals to reference rays is 0.006814813132. The ratio of steps is 0.002248698407.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:32.591
Now the honest failure. The far cascade is coarser, so we mix four probes with bilinear weights.

00:03:32.941 --> 00:03:38.787
If two of those probes sit on opposite sides of a four centimetre wall, occupancy gets mixed.

00:03:39.137 --> 00:03:50.998
We expected the amber closet light to bleed onto the aisle. It did not. Next to the wall, vanilla comes out about ten percent darker than the reference, not brighter, and so does the fix.

00:03:52.198 --> 00:04:01.798
The fix is to march four near intervals, one toward each far probe, merge each pair without mixing occupancy, and only then mix the four results.

00:04:02.148 --> 00:04:12.516
With no extra light to remove, there is no leak for the fix to beat. Both excesses are zero, so gate 11 fails. The wall in this scene simply did not leak.

00:04:12.866 --> 00:04:23.874
A small overlap between shells is a cheaper hack. We measure its error at 0.1851202652. We do not ship it as the photograph.

00:04:25.074 --> 00:04:33.245
This is one two-dimensional floor plan, one software gather on the C P U, six cascades, four rays at the finest shell.

00:04:33.595 --> 00:04:40.528
It does not prove a three-dimensional game engine, and it does not repeat Path of Exile 2 screen space timings.

00:04:40.878 --> 00:04:53.635
Path of Exile 2 uses radiance cascades in screen space, as Sannikov describes in the paper and at ExileCon two thousand twenty-three. That is a cited shipped case, not this run.

00:04:53.985 --> 00:05:07.660
The 256 ray reference against a 1024 ray gather differs by 0.01999415665. The cascade error is larger than that move.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:25.034
Eight gates pass and four fail. The cascade image is about sixteen and a half percent off the reference, just over our fifteen percent target. Gates 6 and 11 fail because the leak we set out to show never appeared, and gate 4 fails because the angle test stayed flat.

00:05:26.234 --> 00:05:27.557
So, the takeaway.

00:05:27.907 --> 00:05:36.077
A penumbra asks for fine positions nearby and fine angles far away. Cascades store those shells. Merging is a window.

00:05:36.427 --> 00:05:49.249
The original is Alexander Sannikov, Radiance Cascades, two thousand twenty-three, with the ExileCon talk, and the later Osborne and Sannikov writeup of the bilinear fix. Thanks for watching.

